# TASK BRIEF: Asuka in her plugsuit

## 1. Mission

You are an autonomous, senior-level and expert-level illustrator/designer. Your assignment is to produce "Asuka in her plugsuit".
Read this entire brief before starting. Statements with MUST / MUST NOT are hard constraints; SHOULD marks strong preferences. If something is ambiguous, choose the interpretation that best matches the quality benchmark and proceed.

## 2. Quality benchmark

Do the work to the production standards, craftsmanship and quality-control level of:
- Kyoto Animation
- Gainax
- Platinum Games (concept arts or CG assets)

### What to emulate

- Their shape language, colour discipline, composition and finishing detail.
- Nothing should look auto-generated or clip-art-like.

## 3. Implementation language / tool

Primary language / tool: SVG (hand-authored)

### Constraints

- Clean, valid SVG 1.1 with a viewBox; no embedded raster images; no external fonts (convert text to paths).

## 4. Concept

### Overall idea

Asuka Langley Souryuu leans to the wall with her back, in relaxed pose, with leg bending in knee, wearing her iconic red plugsuit; dynamical angle (not static, not frontal; the shading is smooth and nothing is flat-looking)
No animation needed

### Comparable existing products (study them; do NOT clone them)

- Huke — borrow: color approach and face stylization

## 5. Age rating & target audience

Age rating: PG-13 — content, themes and tone MUST stay within this rating.

### Target audience

The target audience (for the visual design preferences / aesthetical preferences and preferable player experience): East Asia region user base (NOT westerners; you're catering to Asian preferences entirely, however the language is English only), adult males, East Asia gamers (players of South Korean online games / Chinese mobile games / userbase of Netease etc.), anime and manga fans/otaku and Visual Novel (VN, eroge) players, Japanese console players (PS3, PS4 userbase), audience of anime-adjacent digital art platforms (Pixiv/Gelbooru/Danbooru), users of WeChat/KakaoTalk and the like, users of Bilibili/Douyin/NicoNico etc., 2channel users, people with buddhist/shinto/taoist mentality, etc.

## 6. Features to implement

Implement ALL of the following. Every feature is mandatory unless marked optional.
F01. Strong focal point and clear visual hierarchy
F02. Foreground, midground and background layers
F03. Consistent light direction with highlights and shadows
F04. Key character / object with distinctive silhouette
F05. Subtle gradients and texture (patterns, noise filters) for depth
F06. Named, grouped layers (id attributes) for easy editing
F07. Looks crisp from thumbnail size to 4K

## 7. Visual design & aesthetics

- Style: painterly, implemented in vector (3D-ish, with very thin and sometimes missing outlines); think of it as some 2D game CG (painterly) graphics assets, or a high-resolution icon with masterfully-rendered shapes and highlights/reflected light
- Palette: natural and vibrant colors with defined roles (dominant, secondary, accent, shadow, highlight).
- Line weight: minimal/dynamical
- Composition: square, asymmetrical

## 8. Technical implementation requirements

### Quality standards (MUST)

- Smooth Bézier curves with minimal nodes; no jagged polylines

### Amateurish approaches to AVOID (MUST NOT)

- Auto-traced or blobby shapes with hundreds of nodes
- Random unrelated colours and default 'rainbow' palettes
- Flat, centred, lifeless composition with no depth
- Hidden or off-canvas leftover objects
- Raster images embedded to fake detail

## 9. DO vs DO NOT

Each pair contrasts the expected behaviour with the shortcut that MUST be avoided.
1. DO: Design the silhouette first, then add detail
   DO NOT: Pile on detail over an unreadable shape
2. DO: Limit and assign palette roles deliberately
   DO NOT: Use colours ad-hoc per shape
3. DO: Render the SVG and inspect it at several sizes before delivering
   DO NOT: Assume the code draws what you imagined
4. DO: Deliver complete, working, finished functionality for every listed feature
   DO NOT: Leave stubs, TODO comments, placeholder content or "left as an exercise"
5. DO: Keep the output self-contained and exactly in the mandatory format
   DO NOT: Add extra files, unrequested dependencies or external services

## 10. Minimum scope

The finished product MUST contain at least 30000 lines of SVG code to be considered complete. This size must come from real, meaningful functionality — padding, repetition, dead code or filler does not count.

## 11. Mandatory output format

Deliver exactly: A single .svg file; the filename shall match your identity (the model line, the secondary name and the version, divided with underscores)

### Delivery details

- Canvas [[1080×1080]], transparent or solid background as specified.
- The web app itself shall only display the SVG artwork and nothing else.
